course: world
lesson: 182
title: Sounds wrong, but is right
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Some true answers sound so wrong that people laugh, until they check. These puzzles teach the best habit there is: when your gut and the maths disagree, test it.
explain: Fence posts: a 10-metre fence with a post every metre needs 11 posts, because there is one at 0 as well as one at 10. Count the gaps and the posts separately; the same trap catches log cuts (4 pieces need only 3 cuts) and the snail in the well.
explain: Doubling grows wildly: fold paper and the layers go 2, 4, 8, 16... After 7 folds there are 128 layers, a little paper brick.
explain: Chance surprises us too: a class of 30 has more than 400 different pairs of people, and any pair might match, so two people sharing a birthday is more likely than not.
explain: In the three-cups game, Leo's first pick is right only 1 time in 3, and his sister lifting an empty cup doesn't change that, so switching wins 2 times in 3. Leo: "No way, that's illegal." It isn't: play 30 rounds and count.
fact: In the three-cups game, if the helper knows where the sweet is and always lifts an empty cup you didn't pick, switching wins 2 times out of 3.
fact: Your first pick of three cups is right only 1 time in 3, and the helper lifting an empty cup doesn't change that.
fact: In a class of 30 it is more likely than not that two people share a birthday, because there are more than 400 different pairs of people who could match.
fact: A 10-metre fence with a post every metre needs 11 posts: one at 0 metres and one at every metre up to 10.
fact: A snail in a 10-metre well that climbs 3 metres each day and slides 2 each night is 7 metres up after 7 days and nights, and climbs out on day 8.
fact: Cutting a log into 4 pieces takes 3 cuts; at 2 minutes a cut, that is 6 minutes.
fact: With socks of only two colours, 3 socks are always enough to be sure of a matching pair; grown-ups call this the pigeonhole principle.
fact: Every exact list of 10 fair coin flips, ten heads or any mixed list, has the same chance: 1 in 1,024.
fact: Each fold doubles the layers of paper: 7 folds make 128 layers and 10 folds would make 1,024, which is why ordinary paper is very hard to fold more than about 7 times.
fact: Lightning often strikes the same tall place again; the Empire State Building in New York is hit around 20 times a year.
fact: Words people use: "no way" means "I don't believe it"; "chilling" means relaxing; "my lover" (West Country) is a friendly "dear"; "proper job" (West Country) means well done; "bostin'" (Black Country) means brilliant; "deffo" (UK) means definitely.
exercise: Three cups, a sweet under one. Leo picks cup 1; his sister, who knows where the sweet is, lifts cup 3: empty. Should Leo switch to cup 2? | Yes; switching wins 2 times out of 3 | No; it is exactly 50/50 now | No; staying wins 2 times out of 3 | It makes no difference; the sweet moves
exercise: How often was Leo's first pick right? | 1 time in 3 | About 1 time in 2 | About 2 times in 3 | Every single time
exercise: What must be true for switching to win 2 times in 3? | The helper knows and always lifts an empty cup | The helper lifts any cup at random, even the sweet's | The sweet is always hidden under cup 2 to start | Leo must always pick cup 1 first, every single round
exercise: In a class of 30, how likely is it that at least two people share a birthday? | More likely than not | Almost impossible | Only possible if there are twins | Certain, in every class
exercise: Why is a shared birthday likely in a class of 30? | There are more than 400 pairs of people who could match | Most birthdays bunch up in the same month | Thirty is nearly as big as 365 | Schools put children with the same birthday together
exercise: Dad builds a 10-metre fence with a post every metre. How many posts does he need? | 11 | 10 | 9 | 20
exercise: A snail at the bottom of a 10-metre well climbs 3 metres each day and slides 2 each night. On which day does it get out? | Day 8 | Day 10 | Day 7 | Day 5
exercise: Where is the snail after 7 days and 7 nights? | 7 metres up | 10 metres up | 3 metres up | 21 metres up
exercise: Grandad cuts a log into 4 pieces; each cut takes 2 minutes. How long does it take? | 6 minutes | 8 minutes | 4 minutes | 10 minutes
exercise: A dark drawer holds mixed black and white socks. What is the smallest number you must grab to be sure of a matching pair? | 3 | 2 | 20 | 4
exercise: Kai flips ten heads in a row; his friend gets H T T H T H H T H T. Which exact list was less likely? | Neither; each exact list is 1 in 1,024 | Kai's ten heads | The friend's mixed list | Both lists are impossible
exercise: Why can't you fold a sheet of paper in half many times? | Each fold doubles the layers, so it gets thick fast | Paper gets thinner and thinner with every single fold | Paper always tears after exactly five folds, no more | Each fold adds just one more layer, so it adds up slowly
exercise: How many layers of paper are there after 7 folds? | 128 | 14 | 49 | 64
exercise: Grandma: "Lightning never strikes the same place twice." What is true? | Tall buildings are hit again and again | Lightning always moves on to a new place | Each place is hit only once in a century | Lightning always avoids the tall buildings
test: Leo: "No way, that's illegal." After 30 rounds, switching won about 20 times. What does this show? | Switching really does win about 2 times in 3 | His sister must have been cheating him every round | It is 50/50 and they were just lucky that afternoon | Staying would have won 20 times out of 30 as well
test: What would happen if Leo played 30 rounds and always stayed with his first cup? | He would win only about 10 times | He would win about 20 times, like switching | He would win about 15 times, half of them | He would win all 30 times in a row
test: Two kids in a class of 30 share 14th March. Ruby: "Nah, that's a setup, Miss." What is the fair reply? | It's not a setup; with over 400 pairs, a match is likely | It is a setup; you'd need about 180 kids | It's a fluke that happens 1 time in 365 | Shared birthdays can't happen in small classes
test: Mum: "Ten gaps, Pete, eleven posts." How many posts does a 5-metre fence with a post every metre need? | 6 | 5 | 4 | 10
test: Ollie's sister: "On day 8 it starts at 7 and climbs to 10. It's out, chilling." If the well were only 5 metres deep, on which day would the snail get out? | Day 3 | Day 5 | Day 4 | Day 2
test: Grandson: "You were so ready for 8 minutes, Grandad, you even made a tea." How long would it take to cut a log into 6 pieces at 2 minutes a cut? | 10 minutes | 12 minutes | 6 minutes | 8 minutes
test: Mia: "It IS maths homework." If the drawer held black, white AND red socks, what is the smallest number to be sure of a pair? | 4 | 3 | 6 | 7
test: Friend: "My list is exactly as rare as yours, yours just has a better haircut." What does he mean? | Ten heads only looks special; each exact list is equally rare | Ten heads is far rarer than any mixed list | Mixed lists of flips are impossible | Coins remember what they did last time
test: Ella: "Told ya, it's a paper brick now." How many layers would there be after 8 folds? | 256 | 16 | 128 | 64
test: Gran in Bristol: "Lightning never strikes twice, my lover, everyone knows." What is true? | The Empire State Building is hit around 20 times a year | Lightning never hits the same building again | Lightning only hits places it has never hit | Tall buildings are never hit by lightning
test: In a cartoon a character hides under a tree that lightning has just hit: "Safest spot in the storm, deffo!" Put the cartoon right. | Lightning can hit the same tall thing again, so it isn't safe | A struck tree can never be struck again | Lightning only ever hits flat open ground, never trees or houses | It's safe because the tree is already burnt
exam: It is given that a fence of ten metres in length is to be constructed with a post at every metre, inclusive of both ends. Determine the number of posts required. | Eleven | Ten | Nine | Twenty
exam: A snail situated at the bottom of a well ten metres deep ascends three metres during each day and descends two metres during each night. Ascertain the day on which it emerges from the well. | The eighth day | The tenth day | The seventh day | The fifth day
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a group of thirty persons and the sharing of a birthday. | It is more probable than not that at least two persons share a birthday | It is impossible for any two persons to share a birthday | At least one hundred and eighty persons would be required | The probability is precisely one in three hundred and sixty-five
exam: Determine the minimum number of socks which must be withdrawn from a darkened drawer containing socks of two colours only in order to guarantee a matching pair. | Three | Two | Four | Twenty
